Lloyds Banking Group, 40%-owned by the taxpayer, is coming under pressure from shareholders to sell its Scottish Widows insurance business, which could fetch about £7bn.
Investors argue it will become more expensive for Lloyds to own Widows when new rules being drawn up by the Basel Committee on Banking Regulation force banks to set aside extra funds to protect themselves against another financial meltdown.
Under the new regime to be introduced in 2012/13, banks will be barred from counting capital in their insurance subsidiaries as "common capital" that can also be deployed to cover their mainstream banking operations. The so-called "double counting" of capital will be outlawed as too risky in the wake of the credit crunch.
Several Lloyds shareholders said that selling Widows would shield the bank "from the build-up of pressure for banks to hold more capital to prevent a repeat of the banking crisis of 2008".
A paper published by Matrix Securities argues: "Lloyds fares quite badly under the proposed Basel III regulations... with its Core Tier 1 capital ratio (a key measure of financial health) suffering considerably."
Lloyds Core Tier 1 ratio was 8.1% at the end of last year, but Matrix's Andrew Lim fears it could fall to 6.8% under Basel III in 2012, below the industry norm.
Matrix's view is challenged elsewhere in the Square Mile. Several brokers suggested that the bank will generate surplus capital as the economy recovers and the company deleverages its balance sheet. In this more promising scenario, selling Widows become unnecessary.
Robert Law at Nomura said: "UK banks are benefiting from a combination of declining impairments, rising margins and strengthened capital bases." But he argued that the full value of Widows would be better reflected if it was demerged, with new shares distributed to existing Lloyds shareholders.
Mike Trippett at Oriel Securities says Lloyds can maintain and improve its capital ratios "but the outlook is less certain if we enter a double dip recession and impairments soar."
Lloyds regards Scottish Widows as "a core business". But insiders admit that if Basel III is too stringent, it could reconsider its position.
The bank is hoping for a phased implementation of the proposed rules over a number of years to reduce the impact.
Nomura says that UK banks – which have higher capital ratios than their European counterparts – could sustain their current ratios if they were given three years to adapt to the new regime.
